> ... it's not easy to understand. Maybe this reformulation would help:
>
> the basic model is a logit regression, with the assumption of a
> co-dominant effect of each allele. I.e.:
>
> logit(Disease probability) = Intercept + number of B alleles * log
> OR(B) + number of C alleles * log OR(C) + ....
>
>                                     = Intercept + B * a_1 + C * a_2 +
> D * a_3 + ....
>
> Now I would like to introduce a restriction, that all coefficients
> a_1, ... , a_n come from a common distribution with a_i ~ N( a_mean,
> a_sd^2).
>
> This resembles somehow the iid model of R-INLA, but I wonder how to
> express that correctly to make R-INLA calculate this. The input data
> would look like this, then (example):
>
> Disease    Ntrials   A      B    C   D ....
> yes           3          1       1    0    0                      for
> AB genotype  (== BA genotype!!!)
> no            2           2       0    0    0                     for
> AA genotype
> yes           2          0       1    1    0                     for
> BC genotype
> etc.
>
> I would like to have an odds ratio for disease risk for each allele.
> And I believe that all odds ratios come from a normal distribution.
>
> The more complicated idea is to include phylogenetic information, i.e.
> a tree of genetic distances between  different alleles.
>
> Best regards,
